## Phase 6: Vulnerability Discovery — The Core of CVE Matching

**File:** [provider.py](file:///d:/Work_Stuff/Projects/S-BOM/backend/app/vulnerabilities/provider.py)

This is where CVEs are actually found. The system supports two vulnerability provider modes: **OSV** (default) and **MITRE** (enriched).

### Step 6a: Batch Query to OSV

The scanner takes every extracted component and asks **Google's OSV database** (Open Source Vulnerabilities):

> "Hey OSV, I'm using `axios` version `0.21.1` in the `npm` ecosystem. Do you know of any security bugs?"

**How the API call works** ([_query_batch()](file:///d:/Work_Stuff/Projects/S-BOM/backend/app/vulnerabilities/provider.py#L65-L73)):

```
POST https://api.osv.dev/v1/querybatch

Body: {
  "queries": [
    { "package": {"name": "axios", "ecosystem": "npm"}, "version": "0.21.1" },
    { "package": {"name": "lodash", "ecosystem": "npm"}, "version": "4.17.20" },
    { "package": {"name": "flask", "ecosystem": "PyPI"}, "version": "2.0.1" },
    ... up to 500 packages per batch
  ]
}
```

### Step 6b: How OSV Decides If Your Version Is Vulnerable

OSV stores vulnerability records with **affected version ranges**. Here's a real-world example:

```json
{
  "id": "GHSA-42xw-2xvc-cxqx",
  "aliases": ["CVE-2021-3749"],
  "summary": "Inefficient Regular Expression Complexity in axios",
  "affected": [{
    "package": { "name": "axios", "ecosystem": "npm" },
    "ranges": [{
      "type": "SEMVER",
      "events": [
        { "introduced": "0" },
        { "fixed": "0.21.2" }
      ]
    }]
  }]
}
```

**The matching logic is simple:**

```
Is your version >= "introduced" (0) AND < "fixed" (0.21.2)?

  axios@0.21.1:  0 <= 0.21.1 < 0.21.2  →  ✅ YES, VULNERABLE
  axios@0.21.2:  0 <= 0.21.2 < 0.21.2  →  ❌ NO, this IS the fix
  axios@1.4.0:   0 <= 1.4.0  < 0.21.2  →  ❌ NO, newer than fix
```

> **Key insight:** The vulnerability database doesn't scan your code for bugs. It simply checks: "Is the version number you're using within a range that is known to contain a specific bug?"

### Step 6c: MITRE/NVD Enrichment (Fetching the Full CVE Record)

OSV gives us the **CVE identifier** (like `CVE-2021-3749`), but for the full technical details, the scanner queries MITRE:

```
GET https://cveawg.mitre.org/api/cve/CVE-2021-3749
```

From the MITRE response, [_parse_mitre()](file:///d:/Work_Stuff/Projects/S-BOM/backend/app/vulnerabilities/provider.py#L348-L379) extracts:

| Field | Example | What It Means |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **CVSS Base Score** | `7.5` | How technically severe (0–10) |
| **CVSS Vector** | `CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H` | Breakdown of attack characteristics |
| **Base Severity** | `HIGH` | Human-readable severity label |
| **Description** | "axios before 0.21.2 allows ReDoS..." | What the bug actually does |
| **Fixed Version** | `0.21.2` | What version to upgrade to |
| **References** | NVD links, GitHub advisories | Further reading |

### Step 6d: The Final Vulnerability Record

For each match, a record is created and attached to both the component and the snapshot:

```python
record = {
    "component_id": "uuid-of-axios-component",
    "vulnerability_id": "CVE-2021-3749",
    "source": "mitre",
    "severity": "HIGH",
    "cvss_score": 7.5,
    "cvss_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
    "description": "axios before 0.21.2 allows ReDoS...",
    "fixed_version": "0.21.2",
}
```

